# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR, in one sentence: `wp-content/plugins/` is served by URL,
# and this directory holds two files that have no business being downloadable.
#
# Measured on the mirror on 28/08/2026, before this file existed:
#
#   GET /wp-content/plugins/teeshoop-core/README.md      200, 63 058 octets
#   GET /wp-content/plugins/teeshoop-core/data/garments.json  200, 5 773 octets
#
# The README is the plugin's contract: every hook, every meta key, the order
# lifecycle, the bridge protocol and the reasoning behind the money code. None
# of it is a credential, and that is exactly why it went on being served from
# the day the plugin was created (12/08/2026) to this one. It is a map of the
# shop's internals handed to whoever asks, and the project's rule is that
# anything reachable by URL is public and must be treated as one.
#
# garments.json is the shop's copy of the studio's print areas and size chart.
# It is not a secret (the studio ships the same numbers to every visitor), but
# nothing fetches it over HTTP either: `Garments.php` and the product-specs
# template read it off the disk. A file nobody requests should not answer.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DENIED. `assets/` (the bridge and product CSS and JS)
# is loaded by the browser and must stay served. The PHP files already refuse on
# their own: every file under `includes/` exits on a missing ABSPATH, and every
# file under `tests/` answers 404 unless PHP_SAPI is cli, both measured again on
# 28/08/2026 (200 with an empty body, and 404 with an empty body).
#
# Apache 2.4 syntax, which is what both sides speak: the mirror is Apache 2.4.68
# and o2switch is LiteSpeed, which implements 2.4 htaccess semantics. There is no
# 2.2 fallback because neither host is 2.2, and a stanza nobody can trigger is a
# stanza nobody maintains.

<FilesMatch "\.(md|json)$">
	Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
